(* #FORMAT FormatThese *)

=> Extending Patoline

Patoline is designed to be extended.  There are three layers at which a
user can add functionality:

  1. **The parser.**  The default parser recognises the mixed Wiki /
     LaTeX / OCaml syntax that you have seen throughout this book.
     A custom parser can recognise a different input language and
     lower it to the same OCaml AST.

  2. **The format.**  A format is a set of macros that define how each
     kind of document object (heading, paragraph, list, maths
     environment, ...) is rendered.  Formats are themselves written in
     Patoline and live in the [formats/] subdirectory.

  3. **The output driver.**  A driver consumes the low-level raw
     commands emitted by the format and writes them to a particular
     output (PDF, SVG, HTML, Cairo, ...).

=> The default parser

The default parser is itself an OCaml program built with the Earley
parser combinator library.  It is split into one file per syntactic
construct, all under [pa_patoline/].  Each module exposes an OCaml
value that accepts a stream of tokens and returns an AST.

To add a new construct, the typical workflow is:

  - Identify the grammar rule that catches it.  The simplest cases
    are usually handled in [pa_patoline/pa_parser.ml].

  - Add a lowering function that produces a Parsetree node.  The
    helper module [pa_patoline_ast_helpers.ml] is the single place
    where AST construction lives; please add new helpers there and
    call them from the parser.

  - Rebuild with `dune build` and re-run a sample document to make
    sure the new construct renders.

=> Writing parsers

A custom parser implements the same interface as the default parser:
an OCaml module with type [Pa_main.t].  You can drop it into a
directory and load it via [pa_patoline --package myparser].

A practical example is in [pa_patoline/pa_main.ml], which boots the
default parser and exposes the resulting OCaml entry point to dune.
A complete standalone parser is in the [pa_patoline.ml] executable,
which can be invoked with `pa_patoline file.txp`.

To experiment with new grammars, start with [pa_patoline/pa_lexer.ml]
(the lexer used to feed Earley) and a copy of [pa_patoline/pa_parser.ml].

=> Writing formats

A format is a Patoline program that:

  - Defines macros for all the "block" commands (chapter, section,
    paragraph, item, ...).

  - Sets up the environment (default font, page geometry, ...) at
    the top of the document.

  - Implements the [FormatThese] module signature so it can be
    instantiated by [pa_patoline].

The simplest format is [formats/SimpleSlides.ml], which only does
titles and bullets.  The canonical article-like format is
[formats/FormatArticle.ml].  To write your own, copy [SimpleSlides.ml]
and edit it; every macro is just an OCaml function.

When you write a new format, give it a public name in the [formats/]
dune stanza so it can be loaded as an opam package, and run it
against the existing examples to make sure nothing has regressed.

=> Writing output drivers

An output driver implements the [Patoraw.Driver.OutputDriver]
signature: an [output] and [output'] function that consume a
[page array] and write a file.

  - Look at [drivers/Pdf/Pdf.ml] for the canonical implementation.
  - Look at [drivers/SVG/SVG.ml] for an SVG driver.
  - Look at [drivers/Html/] for the HTML output.

For a new target (for example, PDFium or Harfbuzz), the path is:

  - Add a new directory under [drivers/] with a [dune] file.
  - Register the driver name in [patconfig/patDefault.ml].
  - Build and ship it as an opam package.

Both [DriverPdfium] and [DriverHarfbuzz] already exist as stubs in
[drivers/] and forward to [Pdf] when the underlying C library is not
installed.  See the comment header in each of those modules for
instructions on how to enable the real implementation.

